WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.331 --> 00:00:03.748
[cymbals softly ringing]

00:00:05.690 --> 00:00:07.620
Narrator: Ah, the northern lights.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.488
Beautiful to see...and hear?

00:00:10.488 --> 00:00:12.060
[wind chimes gently ringing]

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:12.893
The thing is,

00:00:12.893 --> 00:00:15.770
the northern lights don't
actually make a sound.

00:00:15.770 --> 00:00:17.240
Yet when you watch this clip,

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.790
you don't think twice about it.

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.117
In fact, if you start to
really look, and listen,

00:00:24.117 --> 00:00:26.793
closely to everything from "Planet Earth"

00:00:26.793 --> 00:00:28.900
to "Our Planet," you'll notice sounds

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.658
that would either be
impossible to capture...

00:00:31.658 --> 00:00:32.683
[Slinky shaking]

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:35.918
or ones that are straight-up made up.

00:00:35.918 --> 00:00:37.193
[leather squishing]

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.930
Nature documentaries are
full of unnatural sounds,

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:43.750
and they're created in postproduction

00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.500
by someone like Richard Hinton,

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.700
whose job as a nature doc Foley artist

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.220
is a study in the subtle
art of exaggeration.

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:54.168
So, why even create
sounds for nature docs?

00:00:54.168 --> 00:00:56.250
[lips smacking]

00:00:56.250 --> 00:00:59.330
Well, shooting in the wild
makes it almost impossible

00:00:59.330 --> 00:01:01.140
to capture real sound.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:04.470
Take this aerial shot of a
dolphin pod in "Our Planet."

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.940
It was filmed high up
in the air by a drone,

00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:09.530
so while the dolphins are
splashing up and down,

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.770
all the camera picks up are loud blades.

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:15.920
Nature docs also frequently
utilize macro lenses,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.410
meaning they might actually
be shooting a subject matter

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:19.870
that's across a valley,

00:01:19.870 --> 00:01:22.480
or they'll capture
objects normally too small

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.850
to have a registered noise to it.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:26.150
That means that sometimes

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:28.630
Richard and his team at Films at 59

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.870
will actually have to fake
sounds to give life to it.

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:33.140
Richard Hinton: You can
fill a television screen

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.680
with a shot of a spider
walking across a web,

00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:35.930
and there's no sound there, obviously,

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:37.830
and that can feel a bit strange,

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:40.102
or there's a hole in the story.

00:01:40.102 --> 00:01:44.290
[magnetic tape rustling]

00:01:44.290 --> 00:01:46.420
Narrator: For "The Hunt,"
he needed to get the sound

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:48.480
of a spider shooting a web.

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.832
Hinton: I really like
doing stuff like this,

00:01:49.832 --> 00:01:51.790
because I get carte blanche
to be a little creative.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.450
Because there's no
perceivable sound in nature,

00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:57.230
it means I can kind of make something up.

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:58.790
Narrator: But while he
can make something up,

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:02.110
he still needs to match the
sound he's watching on screen.

00:02:02.110 --> 00:02:05.130
For the web, he needed
something really stretchy,

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.560
so he grabbed a Slinky
and stretched it out

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.697
as far as possible.

00:02:09.697 --> 00:02:10.720
[Slinky rattling]

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.233
Hinton: You get that
wonderful cable under tension.

00:02:15.770 --> 00:02:16.950
Narrator: Now, it sounded good

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.240
when he just stretched it out

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.530
between his hands, like you see here,

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:23.010
but because the camera was so close,

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:25.260
he needed a really close sound.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:27.200
So he applied proximity effect,

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.000
where the Foley artist will make a sound

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.630
as close to the mic as possible.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:33.417
It's actually a risky move.

00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:35.828
Hinton: The microphone is
almost feeding back on itself

00:02:35.828 --> 00:02:37.750
because you're too close to the mic

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:38.937
or you're overloading the mic.

00:02:38.937 --> 00:02:40.370
Narrator: But he found the winning noise

00:02:40.370 --> 00:02:43.840
only by shoving the Slinky
right on top of the mic.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.750
Hinton: That proximity just
pulls you in really tight

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.110
to the action and what's
going on on the screen,

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.213
and it just makes it sound more unworldly.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:55.920
Narrator: He didn't
just need the web blast.

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:59.440
He also had to capture a
spider's tiny footsteps,

00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:00.790
yet another thing in nature

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:02.640
that normally wouldn't have a sound,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.920
at least that humans could hear.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.570
He needed something more delicate

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.300
than most animal footsteps.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:11.510
He started playing with a
bunch of magnetic audio tape,

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.380
a classic Foley technique that can cover

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.820
many types of sounds.

00:03:14.820 --> 00:03:17.780
Hinton: You could use this,
but it sounds too clumpy.

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:20.430
It's not delicate enough.
It's not precise enough.

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:21.620
If I wrap it around the reel,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.570
I can use the reel as a sounding board.

00:03:24.779 --> 00:03:28.196
[magnetic tape rustling]

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:31.750
And you can get individual footsteps.

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:33.330
Narrator: Close-ups also mean he will need

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.520
to exaggerate parts of a larger animal

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:37.850
you wouldn't normally hear a sound from.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:40.490
For example, when working
on Disney's "Bears,"

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.015
Richard needed to capture the sound

00:03:42.015 --> 00:03:44.760
of a bear opening its eye and blinking.

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.680
Because a bear is covered in fur,

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:50.636
he first rubbed a piece of fur
to capture the lid opening.

00:03:50.636 --> 00:03:52.630
[fur rustling]

00:03:52.630 --> 00:03:55.330
But Richard actually needed
to create two sounds,

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:57.340
as he observed that moisture forms

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:59.290
when one opens their eyes.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:02.855
For a wetter sound, he
relied on his own mouth.

00:04:02.855 --> 00:04:05.410
[mouth salivating]

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.440
It's when these two
sounds are mixed together

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:09.590
that you really hear the lid opening.

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:11.700
[fur rustling]
[mouth salivating]

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.290
The up-close-and-personal
presentation of nature docs

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:15.750
is what brings Richard to create

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.130
some of the most innovative noises.

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:20.320
Thanks to amazing HD cameras,

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.180
time-lapse technology has changed

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.720
what a nature doc can capture,

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.660
and we can now witness the
entire cycle of a plant growing.

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:30.320
Hinton: The beauty, from my
point of view as a Foley artist,

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:32.760
because it is such a stylized shot,

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.990
I don't have to think too carefully

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.700
about being true to nature.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:39.660
It gives me a certain
level of creative freedom.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.080
Narrator: Like with the spider web,

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.710
Richard needed a stretchy sound

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:45.058
for this shot from "Planet Earth II,"

00:04:45.058 --> 00:04:48.240
where mushrooms and other
fungi spring from the ground.

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:51.660
But he wanted a sound more
organic than a Slinky.

00:04:51.660 --> 00:04:55.130
He first went for a mushy
effect with a wet newspaper,

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.892
but he needed more control.

00:04:56.892 --> 00:04:58.242
[newspaper squishing]

00:04:58.242 --> 00:04:59.460
Through this trial and error,

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:01.870
he found leather strips and scuba gear

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:05.547
were the only things both
stretchy and controllable enough.

00:05:05.547 --> 00:05:08.120
[strips stretching]

00:05:08.120 --> 00:05:09.100
To add a little sweetener,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.835
Richard played around
with a wet leather shammy.

00:05:11.835 --> 00:05:12.985
[shammy squishing]

00:05:12.985 --> 00:05:15.170
Hinton: And this is a
particularly useful lad too

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:17.100
if you're showing the decay of the fungus

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:18.660
as well as the growth,

00:05:18.660 --> 00:05:21.840
because usually they kind
of melt into a sticky,

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:24.839
kind of wet, moist,
oozy kind of substance.

00:05:24.839 --> 00:05:29.820
[shammy squishing]
[strips stretching]

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.830
Narrator: Now, there are
certain things in nature docs

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:34.580
that you wouldn't hear even
if you got up close to it,

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:37.720
because they are simply
a visual phenomenon,

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:40.404
like the northern lights
we mentioned earlier.

00:05:40.404 --> 00:05:44.640
[cymbals softly ringing]

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:47.180
For some animal movements
there is a real sound,

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:49.680
but it's not very pleasant to listen to,

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:51.440
so he needs to create something better,

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.457
like he did for these underwater
shots in "Our Planet."

00:05:55.442 --> 00:05:56.860
Hinton: You don't really
hear much sound underwater.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:57.960
Although sound travels very well,

00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.919
it's very [hisses and crackles].

00:06:00.919 --> 00:06:03.030
It would become very tiresome to listen to

00:06:03.030 --> 00:06:04.350
after about two minutes.

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:05.340
Your ears would get really tired,

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:06.200
and you'd probably turn it off.

00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:08.210
So we embellish a little bit.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.570
Narrator: Unsurprisingly,
he uses real water,

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:12.870
but it's how his hands
interact with the water

00:06:12.870 --> 00:06:14.570
that makes the difference.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:15.636
Hinton: The smaller the animal,

00:06:15.636 --> 00:06:17.394
the more delicate you can be.

00:06:17.394 --> 00:06:19.180
Narrator: When it gets
to more medium-size fish,

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.960
like this large shoal of
mackerel in "Our Planet,"

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:25.360
he'll also dump that clump of
magnetic tape into the tank

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.301
for an extra layer of sound.

00:06:27.301 --> 00:06:30.134
[water splashing]

00:06:32.622 --> 00:06:33.622
Hinton: What you can do is

00:06:33.622 --> 00:06:36.690
you can focus on the moving shoal

00:06:36.690 --> 00:06:38.581
and really focus on those tight turns

00:06:38.581 --> 00:06:39.981
and those sharp movements.

00:06:39.981 --> 00:06:42.814
[water splashing]

00:06:44.848 --> 00:06:46.903
Narrator: If there's more than
one type of aquatic animal

00:06:46.903 --> 00:06:48.810
in the shot, Richard will differentiate

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:52.292
by doing one take with the
magnetic tape and one without.

00:06:52.292 --> 00:06:55.125
[water splashing]

00:06:56.360 --> 00:06:59.270
For something larger, like
a whale, seen in this shot,

00:06:59.270 --> 00:07:01.620
he'll both make his movements larger

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.924
and go deeper into the water.

00:07:03.924 --> 00:07:06.590
[water intensely splashing]

00:07:06.590 --> 00:07:08.450
There is always extra editing that goes on

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.180
after Richard is done recording.

00:07:10.180 --> 00:07:12.580
Richard will later apply an EQ,

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:15.580
which filters out harsher
frequency and adds depth,

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:17.240
making it at least feel like

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:19.870
it is really recorded under the ocean.

00:07:19.870 --> 00:07:21.497
Listen to this before...

00:07:21.497 --> 00:07:23.990
[water crisply splashing]

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:25.732
and after.

00:07:25.732 --> 00:07:29.232
[muffled water splashing]

00:07:30.320 --> 00:07:33.350
Lava makes a surprisingly soft sound,

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.190
and when Richard had to exaggerate it

00:07:35.190 --> 00:07:36.990
for this scene in "Planet Earth II,"

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:39.040
he wanted it to have more impact.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.860
Hinton: You kind of hear the interaction

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.350
of what it's setting fire to

00:07:42.350 --> 00:07:44.630
more than the sound of the lava creeping.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.250
Narrator: Now, just the fire crackling

00:07:46.250 --> 00:07:48.730
doesn't give much of a
sense of the massive scale

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:50.150
of a lava flow.

00:07:50.150 --> 00:07:51.820
He needed a low-end rumble.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:54.250
He uses smaller rocks when making glaciers

00:07:54.250 --> 00:07:55.490
to capture the debris

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:57.720
but went for a much larger one here.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:07:58.960
Hinton: For this, I put some gloves on

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:01.388
because there's a real danger
of losing a fingernail.

00:08:01.388 --> 00:08:04.305
[boulder rumbling]

00:08:08.245 --> 00:08:09.930
It's a very bass-y sound.

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.600
Narrator: While Richard
has a lot of freedom

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:13.070
on nature shows and movies,

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:17.060
there's one area he really
can't embellish or exaggerate.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.380
Hinton: There's a lot of science around

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:21.250
animal vocalizations these days.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:24.290
There's a whole range of alarm calls

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:26.870
and distress calls and greeting calls,

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:31.870
so we rely very heavily on
accurate library recordings

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:33.780
to make sure that we're
getting it correct.

00:08:33.780 --> 00:08:34.970
Narrator: But Richard will be brought in

00:08:34.970 --> 00:08:36.770
for the vocals you might not think about,

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:39.560
such as eating, breathing, or panting.

00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:41.720
For "Our Planet,"
Richard was asked to make

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:46.250
a very specific lip-smacking
sound from an orangutan,

00:08:46.250 --> 00:08:48.470
as the actual footage
contained crew members

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.281
talking in the background.

00:08:50.281 --> 00:08:52.948
[lips smacking]

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:00.570
Hinton: And it gets quite painful

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.070
if you're doing that for five minutes,

00:09:02.070 --> 00:09:04.311
but, you know, Foley is a contact sport,

00:09:04.311 --> 00:09:05.612
and we suffer for our art.

00:09:05.612 --> 00:09:10.612
[water bubbling]

